      "id": 1,
      "text": "Carrollton Township was organized as a General Law Township on January 4, 1866.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.carrolltontwp.com/welcome_to_carrollton_township/index.php",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The township’s name honors Dr. Carroll, who began buying land in 1835 and had the post office named after him in 1864.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://puresaginaw.com/a-150-years-of-carrollton-and-where-my-story-begins/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early Carrollton grew around saw mills and salt works on the Saginaw River, with Mershon’s saw mill a landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://puresaginaw.com/a-150-years-of-carrollton-and-where-my-story-begins/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Carrollton is one of Michigan’s smallest townships, covering just 3.34 square miles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrollton_Township%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community once hosted a popular 7‑11 arcade where locals jammed quarters into a Galaga machine.",
